WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.753
Predicting LoRA size

00:00:01.565 --> 00:00:02.779
for any dataset

00:00:03.318 --> 00:00:04.261
A method for

00:00:04.073 --> 00:00:05.216
predicting the LoRA

00:00:05.028 --> 00:00:06.053
rank a dataset

00:00:05.865 --> 00:00:07.138
needs before training

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:07.775
begins

00:00:08.302 --> 00:00:09.304
While there exists

00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:10.153
a large body

00:00:09.965 --> 00:00:10.955
of knowledge on

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:11.474
how to set

00:00:11.285 --> 00:00:12.547
the hyperparameters

00:00:12.359 --> 00:00:13.396
and train LoRA

00:00:13.208 --> 00:00:14.327
effectively2

00:00:14.528 --> 00:00:15.106
3

00:00:15.283 --> 00:00:16.368
it’s still unclear

00:00:16.191 --> 00:00:16.898
how to set

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:17.688
LoRA’s rank

00:00:18.206 --> 00:00:19.456
For larger models

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:20.930
rank-1 can store

00:00:20.741 --> 00:00:21.566
quite a lot

00:00:21.614 --> 00:00:23.064
some datasets like

00:00:22.994 --> 00:00:23.878
RL data

00:00:24.008 --> 00:00:25.128
can be represented

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:25.753
in a small

00:00:25.565 --> 00:00:26.437
number of bits

00:00:26.296 --> 00:00:27.463
and often fit

00:00:27.274 --> 00:00:28.276
within a rank-one

00:00:28.088 --> 00:00:29.125
LoRA adapter

00:00:29.646 --> 00:00:30.660
Full story on

00:00:30.471 --> 00:00:31.367
the dashboard
